<p class="heritage-longread__dek">Suzanna Verboom carried a needle. This is her line &mdash; the maternal line of Rienk Lakeman&rsquo;s wife, traced by Dutch civil records out of a canal village south of Amsterdam back into the eighteenth century. A companion to <a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/family/heritage/lakemans/story' ) ); ?>">the Lakeman story</a>.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-needle-and-the-shears"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>The needle and the shears</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Ter Aar · the Netherlands · 1918 to today</p><p>Every family that crosses an ocean carries something small enough to pack and useful enough to keep. The Lakemans carried a geologist&rsquo;s training and seven languages. The woman Rienk Lakeman married carried a needle.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The woman Rienk Lakeman married carried a needle.</p></aside><p><strong>Suzanna &ldquo;Suus&rdquo; Verboom</strong> &mdash; the name is said <em>Ver-bome</em> &mdash; was born in <strong>Ter Aar</strong>, a canal village in the green heart of Zuid-Holland south of Amsterdam, on <strong>25 September 1918</strong>, six days before Rienk himself. She lived a hundred years exactly, dying in Calgary in 2018. Her father kept a barber shop built onto the house and worked, too, as a tailor; he taught his daughter to sew and to cut cloth, and she became, by her son Martin&rsquo;s account, a most excellent seamstress, a trade she would carry across Venezuela and England and Singapore and set down at last in Alberta.</p><p>For a long time her family&rsquo;s story stopped with that remembered father and a couple of brothers in the same trade. It does not stop there any longer. Dutch record-keeping is among the best in the world &mdash; the parish <em>Doop-, Trouw- en Begraafboeken</em> (baptism, marriage, and burial books) and the civil registration Napoleon imposed in 1811 &mdash; and through it Suzanna&rsquo;s line can now be traced, branch by branch, back into the eighteenth century: to dredgers and dike-workers on the Sliedrecht riverside, to a <em>winkelierster</em> and a <em>dijkwerker</em> in the drained polders of the far north, and to farming-and-fishing families on the islands of Goeree-Overflakkee. This is that line, told as far as the records honestly carry it, and tagged plainly where they run out.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-suzanna-and-her-father-ter-aar-the-early-twentie"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>Suzanna and her father &mdash; Ter Aar, the early twentieth century</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Ter Aar · the tailor-barber on the Kerkweg</p><p>What the family remembers most clearly is <strong>&ldquo;Grandpa Verboom&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; Thomas Cheesman&rsquo;s great-grandfather, and the man who anchors this whole story. He was a <strong>tailor and a barber</strong>, with the <em>kapsalon</em> built right onto the house on the Kerkweg in Ter Aar. He taught his daughter Suzanna to sew and to tailor; she became the seamstress whose skill outlasted every move of her long life. Martin, staying at that house as a boy in the early 1960s, remembered a Holland still half a step out of an older century &mdash; the toilet just off the back door on a covered patio, a chamber-pail in the upstairs bedroom.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He taught his daughter Suzanna to sew and to tailor; she became the seamstress whose skill outlasted every move of her long life.</p></aside><p>The trade ran right through the family. A brother, <strong>Pieter &ldquo;Piet&rdquo; Verboom</strong>, and his wife Kelly kept a hair salon a block away &mdash; Martin remembered the whole household piling onto one oversized Vespa, parents and two daughters aboard. Another brother, <strong>Jacob &ldquo;Yap&rdquo; Verboom</strong>, took over Grandpa&rsquo;s barber shop when the old man retired, so the shears stayed in the family and in the same town. A sister, <strong>Jenny</strong>, with her husband <strong>Jan</strong>, kept the closest tie to the Canadian household: when the Lakemans came back from Singapore in 1974&ndash;75 they stayed with Jenny and Jan and watched the famous open-sky Dutch New Year&rsquo;s fireworks, and the couple later crossed the Atlantic to visit Calgary. One sister, family memory holds, died in infancy.</p><p>The records put a name and a date to that remembered father. On <strong>15 May 1918</strong>, in Haarlemmermeer, a tailor named <strong>Johannes &ldquo;Hannes&rdquo; Verboom</strong> &mdash; twenty-four years old, born in Ter Aar, <em>kleermaker</em> by trade &mdash; married <strong>Leentje Vriesman</strong>; and that same year, family history records, &ldquo;Opa Hannes Verboom&rdquo; opened the barber shop on the Kerkweg. Both the trade and the timing match the remembered grandfather exactly, and the barbering business he founded ran on through his son &ldquo;Yap&rdquo; and into the late twentieth century in the same building. The one document that would seal Hannes as Suzanna&rsquo;s father beyond any doubt, her own September 1918 Ter Aar birth certificate, has not yet been pulled from the archive, so this story names him as her father on strong evidence rather than absolute proof, and says so. (See the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>.)</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-verbooms-of-the-riverside-ter-aar-back-to-sl"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>The Verbooms of the riverside &mdash; Ter Aar back to Sliedrecht</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Ter Aar ← Sliedrecht &amp; Hardinxveld · the riverside Verbooms</p><p>Hannes Verboom was himself the son of a Ter Aar family that had come, a generation or two earlier, from the riverside villages downstream.</p><p>His father &mdash; Suzanna&rsquo;s paternal grandfather &mdash; was <strong>Pieter Verboom</strong>, born in Ter Aar on 7 March 1866, who worked as a <em>brugwachter</em> and <em>wegwerker</em> (a bridge-keeper and road-man) and died about 1941. On 11 July 1889, at Nieuwveen, Pieter married <strong>Suzanna Kooij</strong>, born at Nieuwveen on 24 April 1863, who outlived him, dying at Ter Aar on 21 May 1943. (It is for <em>this</em> grandmother, very likely, that Suzanna was named.)</p><p>Pieter&rsquo;s own father had come from elsewhere. He was <strong>Johannis Verboom</strong>, born in Sliedrecht about 1824 &mdash; one of the riverside dredging-and-shipbuilding villages strung along the Beneden-Merwede, southeast of Rotterdam &mdash; who by the 1880s was living and working in Ter Aar. On 6 August 1852, at Sliedrecht, Johannis married <strong>Pietje van den Bos</strong>. The marriage record names his parents; her father it leaves blank, recording only her mother, <strong>Lena van den Bos</strong>.</p><p>Johannis&rsquo;s parents close this verified stretch of the line. They were <strong>Jan Verboom</strong>, born at Hardinxveld about 1792, and <strong>Pietje van Wijngaarden</strong>, born at Sliedrecht about 1802. Their marriage, on 10 November 1825 at Sliedrecht, is the single richest document in the Verboom branch: it records that Jan was already a widower (of a first wife, Aartje Meerkerk), and it names all four of the next generation up &mdash; Jan&rsquo;s parents as <strong>Teunis Verboom</strong> and <strong>Cornelia Slagboom</strong>, and Pietje&rsquo;s as <strong>Johannis van Wijngaarden</strong> and <strong>Neeltje Nederlof</strong>. Those four, born around the 1760s and 1770s in the Hardinxveld&ndash;Sliedrecht riverside, are the deepest Verbooms the documents reach. This was the front line of the old Dutch war against water. The villages strung along the Beneden-Merwede lived by the river, their men the diggers, dredgers, and dike-builders who deepened the shipping channels, sank the willow-and-stone mattresses that armoured the banks, and kept the low polders behind them from drowning. This was the same waterland that, a few miles upstream at Kinderdijk, set nineteen windmills turning against the flood day and night. What <em>these</em> particular Verbooms did in that world the surviving records do not say; no document in hand assigns any of them a trade, so none is claimed here &mdash; but it was a country that lived or drowned by the river, and they were born into the work of holding it back.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-suzanna-s-mother-the-vriesmans-and-hoflands-of-t"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Suzanna&rsquo;s mother &mdash; the Vriesmans and Hoflands of the northern polders</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Haarlemmermeer ← Petten &amp; Zijpe · polder-diggers &amp; a shopkeeper</p><p>Suzanna&rsquo;s mother, Leentje Vriesman, was born in Haarlemmermeer on 21 February 1895 &mdash; and Haarlemmermeer is itself a clue, because in 1852 it had been a <em>lake</em>, drained dry and thrown open to settlers. The families who farmed and dug it were incomers, and Leentje&rsquo;s were no exception: they came down from the coastal villages of the far north of Noord-Holland.</p><p>Her parents married at Haarlemmermeer on 14 June 1893: <strong>Jacob Vriesman</strong>, a labourer born in Haarlemmermeer, and <strong>Adriaantje Grevenstuk</strong>. Jacob&rsquo;s own father, the elder <strong>Jacob Vriesman</strong>, had come from Petten, the dune village on the North Sea coast; the family&rsquo;s working life there is written right into the records. On 14 September 1828 at Petten, <strong>Cornelis Vriesman</strong> &mdash; Suzanna&rsquo;s great-great-great-grandfather, and a <em>dijkwerker</em>, a dike-worker &mdash; married <strong>Stijntje Hoofd</strong>, a <em>winkelierster</em> (shopkeeper) born across the line at Zijpe. The marriage names their parents: Cornelis was the son of <strong>Jacob Vriesman</strong> (a labourer) and <strong>Neeltje Kroon</strong>; Stijntje the daughter of <strong>Cornelis Hoofd</strong>, a <em>verver</em> (painter), and <strong>Trijntje Duijnmeijer</strong>.</p><p>The other strand of this side, the Hoflands, runs through <strong>Antje Hofland</strong>, who married into the Vriesmans: she was the daughter of <strong>Jan Fekke Hofland</strong>, a <em>boerenknecht</em> (farmhand) born at Barsingerhorn about 1796, and <strong>Guurtje Rijding</strong>, a <em>dienstmeid</em> (housemaid) of Zijpe. These were working coastal people &mdash; dike-workers, farmhands, a shopkeeper, a village painter, every trade small enough to pack and useful enough to keep &mdash; who answered the call of the newly drained Haarlemmermeer and moved south to break new polder ground, exactly as Suzanna&rsquo;s Lakeman in-laws&rsquo; ancestors had once broken the Beemster.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-island-side-the-grevenstuks-and-tiggelmans-o"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>The island side &mdash; the Grevenstuks and Tiggelmans of Goeree-Overflakkee</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Goeree-Overflakkee · the island Grevenstuks &amp; Tiggelmans</p><p>Suzanna&rsquo;s mother&rsquo;s mother, Adriaantje Grevenstuk, came from a different Holland again &mdash; the flat farming-and-fishing islands of Goeree-Overflakkee, in the river-mouth delta of Zuid-Holland, where her uncommon surname is found and almost nowhere else.</p><p>Adriaantje&rsquo;s parents, <strong>Bernardus Grevenstuk</strong> and <strong>Leentje Tiggelman</strong>, married at Dirksland on 2 February 1871; Leentje had been born nearby at Den Bommel on 2 June 1852. The marriage records name the generation above: Bernardus was the son of <strong>Johan Bernardus Grevenstuk</strong> and <strong>Willempje van Eck</strong>; Leentje the daughter of <strong>Hendrik Tiggelman</strong> and <strong>Adriaantje Koppenaal</strong>.</p><p>That Tiggelman&ndash;Koppenaal marriage, on 14 May 1837 at Middelharnis, reaches back further than any other single record in this whole family, naming not just parents but a full rank of great-great-grandparents of the island line (the lineage table and the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a> set out the full roll). The surname appears as both <em>Tiggelman</em> and <em>Tichelman</em> in the records &mdash; the same family, the spelling not yet settled in that era. These island people, born through the later 1700s, are the deepest the maternal records reach: a cluster of delta families rooted in the small world of Goeree-Overflakkee long before any of them dreamed of a daughter in Calgary.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-verbooms-meet-the-lakemans">Where the Verbooms meet the Lakemans</h2><p>The Verboom line runs down through the tailor-barber&rsquo;s daughter, <strong>Suzanna</strong>, who married <strong>Rienk Lakeman Sr.</strong> and crossed the world with him &mdash; Venezuela, Canada, England, Kuwait, Singapore, and home to Calgary. Through her, every branch on this page flows into the polder-and-Indies line she married into, and on to Thomas Cheesman and his three children; that line is told in full in <a href="/family/heritage/lakemans/story/"><a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/family/heritage/lakemans/story' ) ); ?>">the Lakeman story</a> →</a>. The deep Dutch ground it all grew from is set out above. Suzanna&rsquo;s needle, and the trade her tailor-barber father taught her, is the small portable thing this family carried.</p><section class="heritage-tree" aria-label="The Verboom family descent, nine generations from the Sliedrecht riverside to Grande Prairie"><h3 class="heritage-tree__title">The Verboom descent</h3><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Teunis Verboom</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Cornelia Slagboom</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Hardinxveld</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Jan</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Named as Jan&rsquo;s parents in the <strong>1825 Sliedrecht akte</strong> &mdash; the single richest document in the Verboom branch</li><li>Born around the 1760s&ndash;70s on the <strong>Hardinxveld&ndash;Sliedrecht riverside</strong>, the front line of the old Dutch war against water</li><li>Their own baptisms and marriage are tree-cited, not yet imaged &mdash; held <em>probable</em></li></ul></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name">Johannis van Wijngaarden</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Neeltje Nederlof</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Sliedrecht</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Pietje</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Named in the same 1825 akte as Pietje&rsquo;s parents &mdash; the deepest the documents reach on this side</li><li>Sliedrecht river people &mdash; the villages whose men dredged the channels and armoured the banks with willow and stone</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Jan Verboom</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree_