but her own 1918 birth akte has not yet been pulled</li><li>The shears stayed in the family &mdash; Piet kept a salon a block away; Yap took over Grandpa&rsquo;s shop when he retired</li><li>Leentje&rsquo;s people were dike-workers, farmhands, a shopkeeper, a village painter &mdash; the northern polders and the Goeree-Overflakkee islands</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Suzanna Verboom</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Rienk Lakeman Sr.</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Ter Aar → Calgary</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Rienk Jr.<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Alexander &ldquo;Lex&rdquo;<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Martin</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born <strong>Ter Aar, 25 September 1918</strong>, six days before Rienk &mdash; she lived a hundred years exactly, dying in Calgary in 2018</li><li>Her father taught her to sew and cut cloth &mdash; <strong>the needle</strong> she carried through Venezuela, England, Singapore, and home to Alberta</li><li>Through her, every branch on this page flows into the <strong>Lakeman</strong> line &mdash; told in full in its own story</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Martin</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">b. Calgary 1957</span><span class="heritage-tree